les (16 km) distant, and this isolation provides sweeping views of the Teton Range, much of the northern Jackson Hole area as well as the Snake River. Though located adjacent to the Tetons, Signal Mountain was not formed in the same manner or period. The mountain originally was formed by volcanic ashfall from one of the eruptions of the Yellowstone hotspot. The peak is also partially a glacial moraine formed by a receding glacier that came south out of the Yellowstone icecap. This same glacier also created neighboring Jackson Lake.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Signal_Mountain_(Wyoming)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}},{"id":"way/178316923","name":"Amphitheater Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.78113,"lat":43.72977,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q4748387","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphitheater_Lake"},{"id":"way/181023558","name":"Bearpaw Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.72988,"lat":43.83028,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q4876760","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bearpaw_Lake_(Teton_County,_Wyoming)"},{"id":"way/208064139","name":"Bradley Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.75439,"lat":43.71424,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q7348336","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley_Lake"},{"id":"way/519994467","name":"Cirque Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.83578,"lat":43.82971,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q5121963","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirque_Lake_(Teton_County,_Wyoming)"},{"id":"way/181010897","name":"Emma Matilda Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.53094,"lat":43.88532,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q1753991","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emma_Matilda_Lake"},{"id":"relation/1121579","name":"Jackson Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.67226,"lat":43.92092,"ele_m":2065.0,"wikidata":"Q1589175","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jackson_Lake_(Wyoming)","tour":6,"summary":{"extract":"Jackson Lake is in Grand Teton National Park in northwestern Wyoming. This natural lake was enlarged by the construction of the Jackson Lake Dam originally built in 1911, enlarged in 1916 and rebuilt by 1989. As part of the Minidoka Project the top 33 ft (10 m) of the lake is used by farmers in Idaho for irrigation purposes under water rights legislation enacted prior to the establishment of Grand Teton National Park. The lake is the remnant of large glacial gouging from the neighboring Teton Range to the west and the Yellowstone Plateau to the north. The lake is primarily fed by the Snake River, which flows in from the north, and empties at Jackson Lake Dam. Jackson Lake is one of the largest high-altitude lakes in the United States, at an elevation of 6,772 ft (2,064 m) above sea level. The lake is up to 15 mi (24 km) long, 7 mi (11 km) wide, and 438 ft (134 m) deep. The water of the lake averages below 60 °F (16 °C), even during the summer.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jackson_Lake_(Wyoming)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}},{"id":"way/75077718","name":"Jenny Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.73078,"lat":43.76355,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q743054","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny_Lake","tour":2,"summary":{"extract":"Jenny Lake is located in Grand Teton National Park in the U.S. state of Wyoming. The lake was formed approximately 12,000 years ago by glaciers pushing rock debris which carved Cascade Canyon during the last glacial maximum, forming a terminal moraine which now impounds the lake. The lake is estimated to be 256 feet (78 m) deep and encompasses 1,191 acres (482 ha). Jenny Lake is considered to be a major focal point in Grand Teton National Park, with many hiking trails, scenic boat rides, and quick access to the major climbing routes onto the tallest peaks of the Teton Range.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny_Lake","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}},{"id":"relation/1121580","name":"Leigh Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.73607,"lat":43.81225,"ele_m":6877.0,"wikidata":"Q1754014","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leigh_Lake"},{"id":"way/178316920","name":"Surp